    "result": "Excellent! The bug is fixed. Here's what was happening:\n\n**The Problem:** When converting decimal prices to cents (e.g., 39.95 \u2192 3995), JavaScript's floating-point arithmetic produced `3995.0000000000005` instead of exactly `3995`. This failed the `Number.isInteger()` validation check.\n\n**The Solution:** I wrapped the cent calculations with `Math.round()` in the `parseEuros` function:\n- For standard decimal notation: `Math.round(Number(...).toFixed(2) * 100)`\n- For European decimal comma notation: `Math.round(decimalCommaToCents(...))`\n\nThis ensures the result is always a whole number of cents, even with floating-point precision issues.\n\nAll tests pass and your subscriptions (Gym 39.95, Netflix 12.99, Spotify 10.99) now store correctly!",
